{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-48238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3027743251b98968805564e296d7570d80d35ff64b27cf831e631ed9c8efb40a.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-3,0)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-3$ and left-continuous at $x=0.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-3,0].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-3,0]$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-3,0]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[0,1]$ and $[0,4]$ contain the endpoint $x=0,$ but the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\n- $[-1,1]$ and $[-2,2]$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=0,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5fc299953c4d3459843ef49f4e923bb92e5371ee4c594eec7f3fa95c080cf71e"} {"question" "q-48238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[-1,1]"] ["math" "[-2,2]"] ["math" "[-3,0]"] ["math" "[0,1]"] ["math" "[0,4]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[-3,0]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(1,3]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(1,3].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$, $h(x)$,  and $q(x)$ are not right-continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not continuous at  $x=2$, and so is not continuous on $(1,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5fc299953c4d3459843ef49f4e923bb92e5371ee4c594eec7f3fa95c080cf71e"} {"question" "q-48279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/387ed50e8983c83f96ea03bdd7a47d13d33a0c6e0728b52512fef8296351f73e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c5fedec0f96ef81c33146504cdabc8f0400ba79f77591167a285d119d238fd2.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/7f2b4da10350a6ba230e0ec1b0b26822b133ee4a9c1228a61565ed559efcdd64.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/895c64a995d7f07a161a43945c369395b5cb365c5e7ec0f664df130c49795af8.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/09d006953b4fc1d111a84e78fcc6b2038d53768fb82d6f61a0b2a1d0126bbb40.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in [-3,2]$\n- $x\\in (0,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-3,2].$ It is continuous on $(-3,2),$ and also right continuous at $x=-3$ and left continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(0,\\infty )$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5fc299953c4d3459843ef49f4e923bb92e5371ee4c594eec7f3fa95c080cf71e"} {"question" "q-48344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-48344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-76091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-2,\\infty )?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-76091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The correct answer is the following.\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png)\n\n    \n\nIndeed, we have that $h(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-2,\\infty ).$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$\n\n- $q(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not continuous at $x=1,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$\n\n- $g(x)$is not continuous at $x=2,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-76091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5fc299953c4d3459843ef49f4e923bb92e5371ee4c594eec7f3fa95c080cf71e"} {"question" "q-76091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-76091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/27cc3b76baa60c48f103f7d016bb78716330dbb03be791458489e5458bf326bd.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/47dca98b1ac1c7d3310418c3d5eaf58416827aaa5d97d118aa5f3b4a6d59d9f8.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/59bc174fabd3f93321505a54f05832fda46914c8f8f8ad5e88690f53bef3e845.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c56ea94b0d5102c276d7cd4a196517a41611e3f7da82b3ab13c6b404c31cf430.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-76091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-76210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/5e787dda0cccdafaa6a93176daea5f954b1cd1da0beaff410a6563536459208e.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$\n- $x\\in [-2,0]$\n- $x\\in [-1,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-76210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and also left continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-2,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-1,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(-1,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-76210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5fc299953c4d3459843ef49f4e923bb92e5371ee4c594eec7f3fa95c080cf71e"} {"question" "q-76210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-76210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"} {"question" "q-76210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0cc0981735fcd4bb7d0c3ee6653a0dee98fd257c15b258667f5b8571453f37"}] "usage" {"q-48344" [] "q-48238" []} "basis" 1322 "retractions" [[17592186078906 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48238.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y = f(x)$ above continuous?"] [